>> This list is not hosted by the IETF, so could you
>> clarify what the scope and affiliation is?


To prevent confusion regarding the mailing list identity and =
affiliation, the mailing list name and description were updated.


The new submission address is: =
internet-standards-policy@listi.jpberlin.de
	(Replacing: ietf-discussion@listi.jpberlin.de - now obsolete)

The new link is: =
https://listi.jpberlin.de/mailman/listinfo/internet-standards-policy
	(Replacing: =
https://listi.jpberlin.de/mailman/listinfo/ietf-discussion)


The updated list description and disclaimers read as follows:


Internet Standards Policy

Background:
https://nsa.2026.action.cr.yp.to
https://mailarchive.ietf.org/arch/msg/ietf/FsbYnIjnGGX4Eek5DuUiZpc3cBI/

The list archive is public.
To avoid spam, illegal content, and disruption of list operations, the =
mailing list is moderated (usually on a daily basis).

This mailing list operates as an independent private initiative. There =
is no affiliation.

It operates independently of the Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) =
and is neither provided by, nor endorsed by, the IETF. Views expressed =
here are not official IETF positions.

IETF is a registered trademark. For details, see: =
https://trustee.ietf.org/assets/trademarks-and-logos/
Usage here follows the IETF guidelines: "The acronym =E2=80=9CIETF=E2=80=9D=
 may be used factually in any online or traditional publication, in =
presentations and documents, and in press and media reports, as a =
descriptive term for the Internet Engineering Task Force." =
(https://trustee.ietf.org/about/faq/#logo-acronym, October 15, 2026.)


Kind regards,

Ken Kubota

____________________________________________________

Ken Kubota
https://doi.org/10.4444/100



> Am 15.10.2026 um 02:22 schrieb Ken Kubota <ietf@kenkubota.de>:
>=20
> My responses are provided in line with the quoted text below.
>=20
>=20
>> Am 14.10.2026 um 16:02 schrieb Jan Schaumann =
<jschauma@netmeister.org>:
>>=20
>> Ken Kubota <ietf@kenkubota.de> wrote:
>>> For those who disagree with NSA activities at the IETF or wish to =
find solutions for the issues concerning the current IETF situation, you =
can join this mailing list:
>>>=20
>>> https://listi.jpberlin.de/mailman/listinfo/ietf-discussion
>>=20
>> This list is not hosted by the IETF, so could you
>> clarify what the scope and affiliation is?
>=20
> The scope was intentionally kept open to encourage people to join and =
contribute their ideas, wishes, and visions for the future.
> Although the NSA's current activities at the IETF (NSA's SIGINT =
Enabling Project [1]) were the catalyst for creating this mailing list, =
numerous other issues within the IETF remain unaddressed.
> A significant discrepancy exists between the IETF's stated principles =
and its actual practices.
> The internet, and hence the IETF, should serve the end user, as =
outlined in an RFC [2].
> In reality, however, the IETF appears to serve large U.S. companies =
whose interests mirror those of the NSA, such as by erroneously =
asserting a "rough consensus" [3] at the TLS Working Group in a decision =
which effectively weakens internet encryption (Professor Bernstein: "But =
there wasn't consensus; not even close. There also wasn't "rough =
consensus"." [4]).
> The leading German computer magazine already published an article =
concerning the issue of whether the "IETF standardization [is] easy to =
hijack". [5]
> In my opinion, the answer is clearly yes, and moreover, it is evident =
to me that the IETF (or the IETF decision-making process) as a whole has =
been compromised/hijacked. [6]
> Please also read the "Background" links at the mailing list website =
for more information.
> The internet, and consequently internet standardization organisations, =
should serve rather the end user than large companies, and in a =
multipolar world, the equality of peoples, and the equality of =
languages, should be envisioned instead of U.S. primacy / hegemony. =
China, Russia, and India, and their languages, should play a larger =
role.
> Bitcoin (or other means of decentralized payment methods) bypassing =
central banking is important, but seems to have no place in the IETF.
> Although the IETF once undoubtedly delivered valuable outcomes, it has =
unfortunately become a liability.
> Overall, the prevailing sentiment is that certain organizations =
operate more as gatekeepers than enablers.
>=20
> Another purpose of the new mailing list is to bypass IETF censorship, =
as discussed further below.
>=20
> The new mailing list is a private initiative, which means that there =
is no affiliation.
> However, this doesn't exclude the possibility that from the new =
mailing list's activity something new will arise, might it be an =
informal structure or an official organization.
> Of course, compliance with privacy laws will be ensured; no data will =
be passed on to third parties (including a potential future legal =
entity) without explicit permission or legal requirement.
> On the mailing list website it is clearly announced that mails are =
available publicly at the mailing list archive (similar to IETF =
standards).
>=20
>=20
>> I.e., shouldn't discussions about the IETF happen on
>> IETF lists?
>=20
>=20
> Ideally, this would be the case.
> However, there is a significant discrepancy between what the IETF =
promises and what it actually does.
> Professor Bernstein has made this explicit in the case of the SSH =
Working Group chaired by Stephen Farrell and another person: "1. What =
IETF promises" vs. "2. Chairs sabotaging participation" or "3. Chairs =
sabotaging resolution of technical disputes" [7]: "issuing continual =
threats of censorship, presumably scaring many WG participants into =
silence" [7].
> In my opinion, the same holds for the TLS Working Group ("The claim of =
a "rough consensus" was made by a WG Chair composed of three members. =
One of the TLS WG chairs works for an NSA-related company, while another =
one works for a CIA-funded company.") [3].
> Nobel laureate Paul Romer already mentioned that the TLS group =
discussion is dysfunctional, and people "seem to be afraid to speak up =
about the mistakes": "I share the concern that many members voice: the =
group discussion is dysfunctional." [8] "The deep problem is the missing =
messages. People seem to be afraid to speak up about the mistakes." [9]
> Moreover, there is a tendency at the IETF towards total censorship, as =
Professor Bernstein pointed out: "2025.10.05: MODPOD: The collapse of =
IETF's protections for dissent. #ietf #objections #censorship #hybrids" =
[10], see also [11].
> In an absurd decision, the TLS WG has "decided to moderate the =
postings of D. J. Bernstein" [12], whose Curve25519 (X25519 [13]) =
probably runs 90% of today's internet worldwide [14].
> In my case, former NSA lifetime employee Deb Cooley has pleaded for =
removing my publishing rights [15], based on a request by John Mattsson =
from Ericsson (Swedish Wallenberg family). "They have to firmly align =
themselves with the Washington agenda in order to keep Ericsson and Saab =
profitable." [16]
> The Orwellian state of affairs couldn't be more obvious.
> Any discussion of the conflict of interest is framed as and blocked as =
"personal attack."
>=20
> Please direct further questions to the new mailing list, as it is an =
evolving process where everybody may participate freely.
> I also would like to note upfront that answers from my side may take a =
while.
>=20
>=20
> Kind regards,
>=20
> Ken Kubota
>=20
> ____________________________________________________
>=20
> Ken Kubota
