</div>
<div><br><blockquote type=3D"cite"><div>Am 14.10.2026 um 16:02 schrieb =
Jan Schaumann &lt;jschauma@netmeister.org&gt;:</div><br =
class=3D"Apple-interchange-newline"><div><div>Ken Kubota =
&lt;ietf@kenkubota.de&gt; wrote:<br><blockquote type=3D"cite">For those =
who disagree with NSA activities at the IETF or wish to find solutions =
for the issues concerning the current IETF situation, you can join this =
mailing =
list:<br><br>https://listi.jpberlin.de/mailman/listinfo/ietf-discussion<br=
></blockquote><br>This list is not hosted by the IETF, so could =
you<br>clarify what the scope and affiliation =
is?<br></div></div></blockquote><div><br></div><div><div>The scope was =
intentionally kept open to encourage people to join and contribute their =
ideas, wishes, and visions for the future.</div><div>Although the NSA's =
current activities at the IETF (NSA's SIGINT Enabling Project [1]) were =
the catalyst for creating this mailing list, numerous other issues =
within the IETF remain unaddressed.</div><div>A significant discrepancy =
exists between the IETF's stated principles and its actual =
practices.</div><div>The internet, and hence the IETF, should serve the =
end user, as outlined in an RFC [2].</div><div>In reality, however, the =
IETF appears to serve large U.S. companies whose interests mirror those =
of the NSA, such as by erroneously asserting a "rough consensus" [3] at =
the TLS Working Group in a decision which effectively weakens internet =
encryption (Professor Bernstein: "But there wasn't consensus; not even =
close. There also wasn't "rough consensus"." [4]).</div><div>The leading =
German computer magazine already published an article concerning the =
issue of whether the "IETF standardization [is] easy to hijack". =
[5]</div><div>In my opinion, the answer is clearly yes, and moreover, it =
is evident to me that the IETF (or the IETF decision-making process) as =
a whole has&nbsp;been compromised/hijacked. [6]</div><div>Please also =
read the "Background" links at the mailing list website for more =
information.</div><div>The internet, and consequently internet =
standardization organisations, should serve rather the end user than =
large companies, and in a multipolar world, the equality of peoples, and =
the equality of languages, should be envisioned instead of U.S. primacy =
/ hegemony. China, Russia, and India, and their languages, should play a =
larger role.</div><div>Bitcoin (or other means of decentralized payment =
methods) bypassing central banking is important, but seems to have no =
place in the IETF.</div><div>Although the IETF once undoubtedly =
delivered valuable outcomes, it has unfortunately become a =
liability.</div><div>Overall, the prevailing sentiment is that certain =
organizations operate more as gatekeepers than =
enablers.</div><div><br></div><div>Another purpose of the new mailing =
list is to bypass IETF censorship, as discussed further =
below.</div><div><br></div><div>The new mailing list is a private =
initiative, which means that there is no affiliation.</div><div>However, =
this doesn't exclude the possibility that from the new mailing list's =
activity something new will arise, might it be an informal structure or =
an official organization.</div><div>Of course, compliance with privacy =
laws will be ensured; no data will be passed on to third parties =
(including a potential future legal entity) without explicit permission =
or legal requirement.</div><div>On the mailing list website it is =
clearly announced that mails are available publicly at the mailing list =
archive (similar to IETF =
standards).</div><div><br></div></div><div><br></div><blockquote =
type=3D"cite"><div><div>I.e., shouldn't discussions about the IETF =
happen on<br>IETF =
lists?<br></div></div></blockquote></div></div><div><br></div><div><div>Id=
eally, this would be the case.</div><div><div>However, there is a =
significant discrepancy between what the IETF promises and what it =
actually does.</div><div>Professor Bernstein has made this explicit in =
the case of the SSH Working Group chaired by Stephen Farrell and another =
person: "1. What IETF promises" vs. "2. Chairs sabotaging participation" =
or "3. Chairs sabotaging resolution of technical disputes" [7]: "issuing =
continual threats of censorship, presumably scaring many WG participants =
into silence" [7].</div></div><div>In my opinion, the same holds for the =
TLS Working Group ("The claim of a "rough consensus" was made by a WG =
Chair composed of three members. One of the TLS WG chairs works for an =
NSA-related company, while another one works for a CIA-funded company.") =
[3].</div><div>Nobel laureate Paul Romer already mentioned that the TLS =
group discussion is dysfunctional, and people "seem to be afraid to =
speak up about the mistakes": "I share the concern that many members =
voice: the group discussion is dysfunctional." [8] "The deep problem is =
the missing messages. People seem to be afraid to speak up about the =
mistakes." [9]</div><div>Moreover, there is a tendency at the IETF =
towards total censorship, as Professor Bernstein pointed out: =
"2025.10.05: MODPOD: The collapse of IETF's protections for dissent. =
#ietf #objections #censorship #hybrids" [10], see also =
[11].</div><div>In an absurd decision, the TLS WG has "decided to =
moderate the postings of D. J. Bernstein" [12], whose Curve25519 (X25519 =
[13]) probably runs 90% of today's internet worldwide [14].</div><div>In =
my case, former NSA lifetime employee Deb Cooley has pleaded for =
removing my publishing rights [15], based on a request by John Mattsson =
from Ericsson (Swedish Wallenberg family). "They have to firmly align =
themselves with the Washington agenda in order to keep Ericsson and Saab =
profitable." [16]</div><div>The Orwellian state of affairs couldn't be =
more obvious.</div><div>Any discussion of the conflict of interest is =
framed as and blocked as "personal =
attack."</div><div><br></div><div>Please direct further questions to the =
new mailing list, as it is an evolving process where everybody may =
participate freely.</div><div>I also would like to note upfront that =
answers from my side may take a =
while.</div></div><div><br></div><div><br></div><div>Kind =
regards,<br><br>Ken =
Kubota<br><br>____________________________________________________<br><br>=
Ken Kubota<br><a =
href=3D"https://doi.org/10.4444/100">https://doi.org/10.4444/100</a><br><d=
